          "title": "You'll want to know: incolumis, incolume",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "incolumis, incolume — \"unharmed, safe\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Omnēs nautae incolumēs ad lītus pervēnērunt. All the sailors reached the shore unharmed."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two endings. Caesar uses it of soldiers who came back safe and sound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "incolumis joins in-, \"not\", and a second part that EtymDB traces to Proto-Italic kadami-, \"damaged\". Lewis & Short compare calamitās; the details are not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
